            <ArticleTitle>Acute myocardial infarction in a patient with primary coronary dissection and severe coronary vasospasm. A case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>951-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A case of myocardial infarction associated with coronary artery dissection in a thirty-five-year-old woman is reported. An emergent coronary angiogram revealed extensive dissection and thrombosis in the right coronary artery; in addition, severe vasospasm was observed in the left coronary artery on the next day. She was successfully treated with intracoronary thrombolysis and intra-aortic balloon pumping. The patient is alive and well two years after infarction.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
